@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — searches carried and what came back
#
# The first instances of pm:Searching. The first two are from the run of
# 2026-09-09, the ones after them from the runs that followed; the file grows
# by appending and the order is the order in which the searches were carried.
# They are here rather than in a directory of their own because a search is a
# protocol about the work, like the examinations beside them, and a new data
# directory would have to be registered in five places for two nodes.
#
# The first is the case the class exists for: a question put to a corpus that
# answered by staying silent. Before there was a class for it, that finding
# lived in an issue label and never reached anyone loading the graph.
#

pmp:FourthWayStatesAntecedentSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Fourth Way names an antecedent for its four states"@en, "Ob der Vierte Weg für seine vier Zustände einen Vorgänger nennt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror)" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Whether the work derives its fourfold ordering of states of consciousness from an earlier source, as it derives its fourfold division of bodies from four named terminologies at pmc:FourBodiesAcrossTerminologies." ;
    pm:searchTerms "Full-text search of the whole work for: Theosoph (ten occurrences, all concerning bodies, planes or societies); Vedanta, Vedantic; turiya, Turiya; Mandukya; Upanishad." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-09" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-09" ;
    skos:note "The finding is a silence and not a refutation. The work names no antecedent for the states, while naming four terminologies for the bodies in the same volume; a route to the theosophical or Vedantic material may still exist outside it. What the node records is that this corpus was asked and did not supply one, which is a different thing from nobody having asked. See prima-materia#653."@en ;
    skos:note "Its weight is the weight of the terms above and no more. A search in Latin transliteration finds nothing about a doctrine the author might have named in Russian, and the scanned text is an OCR whose failures are unknown. Anyone with a better copy can overturn this in an afternoon, which is the point of writing it down."@en .

pmp:MorallyPolarisedBinarySearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether a pre-colonial Indian source polarises the two methods morally"@en, "Ob eine vorkoloniale indische Quelle die beiden Methoden moralisch polt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Kālikāpurāṇa (text, introduction and translation in English by B. N. Shastri, part III, Nag Publishers, Delhi 1991)" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A pre-colonial Indian passage presenting dakṣiṇa and vāma as a clean two-way division with a moral polarity, using union with the divine against self-deification as the criterion that separates them — the falsifying condition of pmp:BinaryPredatesItsReportersExamination." ;
    pm:searchTerms "Full-text search of the translation for: vama, vamacara, vamachara, daksina, daksinacara; left hand, right hand, left-hand, orthodox, heterodox." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:KalikapuranaLeftAndRightMethods ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-09" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-09" ;
    skos:note "The search was for the polarity and found the pair without it. That is the productive outcome and the one an unrecorded search loses entirely: a run that only enters what it set out to find reports its own question back to itself. The passages became pmc:KalikapuranaLeftAndRightMethods and the four nodes around it."@en ;
    skos:note "One corpus, one translation, one spelling set. The corpora that would carry the polarity if anything does — the Kaula tantras, the Śākta digests of the Bengal schools — were not opened for this question, and this node says so rather than letting the silence of the tracker say it."@en .

#
# Two searches of 2026-09-10, both at the same work and both about the hand.
# They were owed from the run of 2026-09-09, whose results reached an issue
# comment and nobody loading the graph; they were carried again rather than
# transcribed, so that what these nodes report is a search and not a report of
# a search.
#

pmp:UptakeThroughTheHandSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Baopuzi has qi taken up out of another person through the hand"@en, "Ob der Baopuzi ein Aufnehmen von Qi aus einem anderen Menschen über die Hand kennt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian, twenty juan, in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185; searched over the whole work in the Chinese, and read at the wording where a hit fell" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage in which qi, energy or vitality is taken up out of another person, with the hand as the place of entry — the claim of prima-materia#328, whose second half is that the entry point is the left hand." ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of all twenty juan for: 掌 (palm, seven hits, all figurative or lexical: ruling in the palm, holding in the palm, dragon's liver and tiger's palm as a drug name); 掌心 and 手心 (palm centre, none); 勞宮 (the point in the palm centre, none); 取人 (four hits, all about taking a man's position, wealth or wife, in the list of transgressions of juan 6 and in juan 10); 受人, 布炁, 奪人炁 (none); 以手 (one hit, juan 17); 摩 (rubbing, five hits, all pharmaceutical or lexical)." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-10" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-10" ;
    skos:note "The direction is the finding, and it is uniform. Every operation in this work in which the hand and the qi appear together sends outward: the blowing over one's own hand at a distance in juan 8, and the subduing of a snake in juan 17, where the practitioner faces the sun, takes three breaths and holds them, props the tongue against the palate and pinches a hand posture. Nothing takes anything in, and nothing takes anything out of a person. A transmission that describes the outward operation in detail and never mentions the inward one is saying something, and it is not the same thing as a transmission nobody has asked."@en ;
    skos:note "What the search cannot say. It was carried in the Chinese over one edition and one set of characters, and a technical term for the intake that this vocabulary does not contain would not have been found. The one result that would carry beyond this work is the absence of 勞宮 anywhere in it, since that is the term the claim would be expected to attach to; but the term belongs to the medical corpus, and its absence here bears on this work only."@en ;
    skos:note "See prima-materia#328. The issue keeps its korpus:daoistisch label and stays open, because the label is the record of coverage and this search adds one corpus to it rather than exhausting the plausible ones."@en .

pmp:PalmsAsSendingOrganSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Baopuzi makes the palms the organ of sending"@en, "Ob der Baopuzi die Handflächen zum Sendeorgan macht"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8; read at the wording, with the remaining nineteen juan searched by term" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage making the palms the place at which the qi leaves the body in an operation on another person or thing — the claim of prima-materia#314." ;
    pm:searchTerms "The whole of juan 8 read at the wording; term search over all twenty juan for 掌, 掌心, 手心, 勞宮, 出氣, 發氣, 以手." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:XuZhu ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-10" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-10" ;
    skos:note "The chapter has the hand in exactly one operation and puts it in the other role. The practitioner who has heard that someone at a distance was struck by a venomous creature blows and incants over his own hand, the left for a man and the right for a woman, and the other is healed though a hundred li away. The hand is what the blowing is directed at and not what does the sending; the sending is the blowing, which the same passage uses on water, on fire, on a tiger and on a wound with no hand in it at all. What the search found is a near neighbour of the claim that assigns the office elsewhere."@en ;
    skos:note "The laterality is the part of the find that matters to the issue, and it points away from it as well. Left and right are distinguished by the sex of the patient and not by a function of the two hands, so the passage cannot be cited for a rule about which hand transmits. The node it yielded carries the wording."@en ;
    skos:note "Recorded as an outcome and not as a refutation. The claim may still stand in the corpora the issue names beside this one; what this node says is that this work was asked and answered with something else. See prima-materia#314."@en ;
    skos:note "Added after the seventeenth juan was read at the wording later the same day: the rule stands there a second time, in a different operation. Blowing the qi at a snakebite ten and more li away, the practitioner calls the man's surname and name and invokes over his own left hand for a man and his own right for a woman, exactly as in the eighth. What the search found is therefore not a formula of one chapter but a rule the work sets twice, and the office it assigns to the hand is the same both times."@en .

#
# A search of 2026-09-10 in the medical canon, carried while the corpus was
# open for other questions. It is the case in which the outcome that had been
# reported was a silence and the search itself found a place.
#

pmp:GeneralRuleAgainstExcessSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the medical canon states a general rule against excess of practice"@en, "Ob der medizinische Kanon eine allgemeine Maßregel gegen das Übermaß der Praxis nennt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Huangdi neijing, Suwen in the Chongguang buzhu recension and Lingshu in the Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, both in the Sibu congkan facsimile; searched over both classics in the Chinese, with the chapters carrying a hit read at the wording" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A general rule, not a single case, that too much of a prescribed practice injures and that a measure is therefore to be kept — the claim of prima-materia#240, which reaches this corpus among five." ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of both classics for: 過度 (six hits, all about excess of exertion or of pleasure and cold and heat, none about a practice); 無過其度 (one hit, Lingshu 61); 五勞所傷 (Suwen 23); 久視傷 (Lingshu 78, the same five clauses); 不妄作勞 (Suwen 1)." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:WulaoSuoshang ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-10" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-10" ;
    skos:note "The result is a general rule and not the general rule the claim asks for, and the difference is worth keeping. The five exhaustions state that a thing done too long injures, name the injury in each case, and stand twice in the corpus; but the five things are looking, lying, sitting, standing and walking, which are postures and not prescribed techniques. The corpus's own rule about its own operation is a single clause with no quantity and no consequence, and it is entered separately."@en ;
    skos:note "What the search settles for this corpus is therefore narrower than the issue asks and sharper than a silence would have been. The transmission does have the form of claim the question is after, applies it to ordinary conduct, and declines to give it any content where its own technique is concerned. That asymmetry is a finding about the corpus and would not have been visible from either half alone."@en ;
    skos:note "Written a day after the search, because the node it names could not be reached from any branch on which it would have validated. The guard in tests/test_publication.py refused a part that points at a node it does not name, which is the condition of SPEC §1 and which held. What that shows about this class is worth keeping: a pm:Searching with a find is bound to the node it yields and travels with it, so such a search belongs in the same change as its find and not in a batch of searches."@en ;
    skos:note "See prima-materia#240. The issue keeps its korpus:huangdi-neijing label and stays open, because four further corpora named there have not been asked."@en .

pmc:FushiSuccessSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether anybody is reported as having lived by the mineral elixirs"@en, "Ob jemand als durch die Mineralelixiere lebend berichtet wird"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian, twenty juan, in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185; and Han Yu, Wubaijia zhu Changli wenji, forty-one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text. Whole-text search over both, in the Chinese."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "The counter-search owed by the protocol at pmc:YiyaoBaizhe: a report, in either corpus, of a named person who took a mineral elixir and lived long or did not die of it, which is the outcome the case series says did not occur."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "Searched for the terms for obtaining immortality, for ingesting drugs, for mineral drugs, for sulphur and for the golden elixir. The first is frequent across the Baopuzi and the places naming a person together with an elixir are in juan 4; in the Han Yu collection the terms for ingesting drugs, for mineral drugs and for sulphur occur only at the epitaph itself and at the poem to the supernumerary of Suizhou."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:YinjunDexian ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-10" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-10" ;
    skos:note "Found in the transmission: exactly one claimed success, and it is the one the fourth chapter offers as its whole evidence. A scholar of the end of the Han is said to have become an immortal by the Great Clarity elixir and to have written down more than forty others he knew of. It is second-hand by its own account, reported by an author who states in the same chapter that he never compounded the drug, and it is held at pmc:YinjunDexian."@en ;
    skos:note "Found in the witness's own collection, and unlooked for: a poem in which the author of the case series begs a friend for a knife-point of the golden elixir to save his sick body. The commentators of the edition argue from it over whether he took mineral drugs himself, and the dispute is held at pmc:HanyuFushiDispute. This is why a counter-search is run over the witness and not only over the claim."@en ;
    skos:note "What the search did not find is worth saying: in neither corpus is there a named person reported as having taken a mineral elixir and lived out an ordinary span in good health. The positive cases in the Baopuzi are immortals, that is to say persons who are said to have left the ordinary span altogether, and no report was located of the intermediate outcome the transmission also promises at half an ounce of the golden liquor, in which the taker keeps wife, children and office."@en .
